our OB leads a human hormone a  prescription medicine or a deadly  narcotic actually it can be all three in  fact  opioids are not so foreign they're found  in our bodies secreted by the pituitary  and adrenal glands endogenous opioids  are neurotransmitters that our body  synthesizes to regulate our response to  stimuli things like emotions motivation  and pain are all regulated by endogenous  opioids exogenous opioids as a prefix  EXO suggests or opioids not made or  found in our body it can be natural like  morphine semi synthetic like heroin or  entirely synthetic like naloxone both  types of opiates bind to G  protein-coupled receptors the activation  of g proteins is what causes the effect  of opioids here is an example of a mu  receptor with an agonist bound to the  active site broadly speaking when  opioids bind and activate GPCRs the  activated GPCRs inhibit the transmission  of a neuronal signal from one neuron to  another one way in which opioids  modulate neuronal signaling is through  postsynaptic inhibition as the opioids  bind to GPCRs colored in red potassium  channels are opened and the positive  potassium ions leave the neuron thus  leaving no signal to be relayed to the  next neuron zoomed in we see the  activated g-protein release it's G beta  gamma complex which activates the  potassium channel and causes  hyper-polarization naloxone is a  synthetic derivative of oxymorphone  intended to reverse the antagonizing  effects of opioids in action while on  the locks on replaces the opioids bound  to gpcrs due to its higher affinity it  doesn't activate the g-protein every day  130 plus people die from an opioid  overdose ten point three million people  misused prescriptions just in 2018 if  there's one thing you can do to address  the epidemic avoid opioid derived  prescriptions and recognize that  treatment not punishment is the answer  you
 

